' =====================================================================
'  MathGen training  -  beginner-friendly "simulation" of the loop
'  Source of truth: src/train.py
'  Two diagrams in one file:
'    A) Activity   -  ONE optimizer step (the inner micro-batch cycle)
'    B) Sequence   -  the higher-level epoch loop + actors
'  Render:
'    java -jar plantuml.jar -tpng training-loop.puml
'    java -jar plantuml.jar -tsvg training-loop.puml
' =====================================================================

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' DIAGRAM A  -  ACTIVITY: one optimizer step (inner training loop)
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@startuml training-loop-activity
title MathGen  -  Inner Training Loop (how ONE weight update happens)

skinparam backgroundColor #FDFDFD
skinparam ActivityBorderColor #2C3E50
skinparam ActivityBackgroundColor #EAF2F8
skinparam ActivityDiamondBackgroundColor #FCF3CF
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FEF9E7
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"

start

note right
  A "batch" = 32 example problems.
  The model tries to guess each
  next token; we measure how wrong
  it is, then nudge the weights.
end note

:Get a batch (32 examples) from DataLoader\n(x = the text so far,  y = the correct next token);

partition "fp16 AMP wraps forward + backward\n(autocast saves VRAM, GradScaler keeps math stable)" #F4ECF7 {
  :Forward pass:  model(x)  ->  logits\n(the model's guess for the next token);
  :Compute cross-entropy LOSS\n(compare logits vs target next-token y);
  :loss = loss / grad_accum (2)\n(we sum 2 micro-batches, so scale each down);
  :Backward pass:  loss.backward()  ->  gradients\n(for every weight: which direction reduces the loss?);
}

note left
  WHAT JUST HAPPENED:
  * LOSS  = HOW WRONG the model is (one number).
  * BACKPROP = WHICH DIRECTION to adjust each of
    the ~35M weights (a gradient per weight).
  * The OPTIMIZER then takes the actual step.
end note

if (Accumulated 2 micro-batches?) then (no)
  ->  not yet  -  keep collecting gradients;
  :(gradients add up, no weight change yet);
  detach
else (yes)
endif

:Clip gradients to max norm 1.0\n(stops one giant update from blowing things up);
:Optimizer step:  AdamW updates ALL weights\n(takes the actual step downhill);
:Cosine-warmup scheduler sets the learning rate\n(small at first, then smoothly decays);
:Zero the gradients\n(wipe the slate clean for the next step);

note right
  One full pass through here = ONE optimizer step
  (= 1 effective batch of 64 examples).
  Then we loop back to "Get a batch".
end note

:loop back to "Get a batch">

stop
@enduml


' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
' DIAGRAM B  -  SEQUENCE: the epoch loop + actors
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------
@startuml training-loop-sequence
title MathGen  -  Epoch Loop (the big picture over 10 epochs)

skinparam backgroundColor #FDFDFD
skinparam defaultFontName "Segoe UI"
skinparam sequenceArrowColor #2C3E50
skinparam sequenceLifeLineBorderColor #5D6D7E
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #EAF2F8
skinparam actorBackgroundColor #D5F5E3
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FEF9E7

actor "train.py" as T
participant "DataLoader"            as DL
participant "MathGen model"         as M
participant "Loss\n(cross-entropy)" as L
participant "AdamW optimizer"       as O
participant "Validation set"        as V
participant "Checkpoint\n(best.pt)" as C

note over T
  We repeat the whole thing 10 times.
  Each repeat = 1 "epoch" = the model
  sees the entire training set once.
end note

loop 10 epochs
  note over T, O
    Train on every batch, learning a little each step.
  end note

  loop over batches (the inner loop from Diagram A)
    T  -> DL : ask for next batch (32 examples)
    DL --> T : x (input),  y (correct next token)
    T  -> M  : forward pass  model(x)
    M  --> T : logits (the guess)
    T  -> L  : cross-entropy(logits, y)
    L  --> T : loss  (how wrong)
    T  -> M  : loss.backward()  -> gradients
    note right of M : gradients = which way\nto nudge each weight
    T  -> O  : every 2 micro-batches: step() + zero_grad()
    O  --> M : updated weights
  end

  == End of epoch: check progress on UNSEEN data ==

  T -> V  : run model on the validation set
  V -> M  : forward only (no learning here)
  M --> V : predictions
  V --> T : val_loss  (how wrong on data it never trained on)

  alt val_loss < best_val  (we improved!)
    T -> C : save best.pt  (this is our best model so far)
    C --> T : saved
  else not better
    T -> T : keep the previous best.pt
  end
end

note over T, C
  After training, eval_model.py loads best.pt and checks the model
  actually generates VALID JSON.
  Low loss alone does NOT prove the model is good  - 
  a real test is whether its output is usable.
end note

@enduml
